        <section id="section8" class="faq-section">
            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>What is the difference between standard aerosol filling and Bag-on-Valve (BoV) for sunscreens?</h4>
                <p>Standard aerosol filling mixes the active sunscreen formulation directly with the propellant inside the pressurized can, spraying them together. Bag-on-Valve (BoV) system places the formulation inside a multi-layered bag, while compressed air or nitrogen surrounds the bag to squeeze the formulation out, keeping the sunscreen active separate from the propellant gas.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Which propellant is best for a transparent chemical aerosol sunscreen?</h4>
                <p>Liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) blends or Dimethyl Ether (DME) are commonly used when the sunscreen active concentrate must be dissolved directly in the propellant for a clear spray. However, DME requires careful can lining selection due to its strong solvent characteristics.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Why are physical UV filters like Zinc Oxide difficult to formulate in standard aerosol sprays?</h4>
                <p>Physical mineral filters are insoluble metal oxides that form particulate suspensions. In a standard aerosol can, these particles tend to settle and agglomerate over time, which frequently clogs the fine channels of continuous valves and actuators, resulting in packaging failure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Is corrosion testing mandatory for water-based aerosol sunscreens?</h4>
                <p>Yes, water-based formulations, particularly those containing electrolytes or inorganic salts, represent a highly corrosive environment for tinplate and aluminum cans. Accelerated corrosion stability testing at elevated temperatures (typically 40°C) is mandatory to select appropriate internal epoxy-phenolic linings.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>How are flammability concerns managed during sunscreen manufacturing?</h4>
                <p>Sunscreen formulations containing high concentrations of ethanol or liquefied propellants like LPG are highly flammable. Manufacturing plants must utilize explosion-proof filling rooms with class 1 division 1 ratings, pneumatic valves, active gas detection sensors, and high-velocity ventilation systems.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>What are the minimum order quantities (MOQs) for contract filling sunscreen sprays?</h4>
                <p>Due to the complexity of setting up high-speed aerosol lines and gassing equipment, most reputable contract packaging manufacturers enforce a minimum order quantity (MOQ) of 10,000 to 25,000 units per production run.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

